        <item>
    <title>Conditional vs Children Rendering Issue</title>
    <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/conditional-rendering-vs-children-rendering/</link>
    <pubDate>Sat, 30 May 2026 14:50:18 -0300</pubDate>
    <author>alexandersonmbm@gmail.com (Alex Morte)</author>
    <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/conditional-rendering-vs-children-rendering/</guid>
    <description><![CDATA[<h3 id="introduction">Introduction</h3>
<p>Conditional rendering and children rendering are two different approaches to render components in react.
Not all <strong>children rendering</strong> are conditionals, but they can and that&rsquo;s when things get odd.</p>
<p>Even though we can achieve similar (almost equal) results, react renders them in a different way. Lets analyze the following example (Full code at the end).</p>
<p>Using conditional render, we have:</p>
<div class="code-block code-line-numbers open" style="counter-reset: code-block 0">
    <div class="code-header language-typescript">
        <span class="code-title"><i class="arrow fas fa-angle-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
        <span class="ellipses"><i class="fas fa-ellipsis-h" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
        <span class="copy" title="Copy to clipboard"><i class="far fa-copy" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
    </div><div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"><code class="language-typescript" data-lang="typescript"><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kr">import</span> <span class="nx">React</span> <span class="kr">from</span> <span class="s1">&#39;react&#39;</span><span class="p">;</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kr">const</span> <span class="nx">Countdown</span>: <span class="kt">React.FC</span><span class="p">&lt;</span><span class="nt">CountdownProps</span><span class="p">&gt;</span>  <span class="o">=</span> <span class="p">({</span><span class="nx">initialCount</span><span class="p">})</span> <span class="o">=&gt;</span> <span class="p">{</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="kr">const</span> <span class="p">[</span><span class="nx">model</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="nx">setModel</span><span class="p">]</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="nx">React</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">useState</span><span class="p">&lt;</span><span class="nt">CounterModel</span> <span class="err">|</span> <span class="na">null</span><span class="p">&gt;(</span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">);</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="k">return</span> <span class="p">&lt;</span><span class="nt">p</span><span class="p">&gt;</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="p">{</span><span class="nx">model</span><span class="o">?</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="nx">value</span> <span class="o">??</span> <span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">}</span> <span class="c1">// Conditional rendered
</span></span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="c1"></span>    <span class="p">&lt;/</span><span class="nt">p</span><span class="p">&gt;</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="p">}</span></span></span></code></pre></div></div>
<p>This example works just fine. <code>model</code> is initially null and we have a blank screen. So far,
nothing new. However, if we use children rendering:</p>]]></description>
